s subtract multiples of $2\\pi $ from $\\frac{7\\pi }{3}$:\n\n- $\\frac{7\\pi }{3}-2\\pi =\\frac{\\pi }{3}$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{7\\pi }{3}-2\\cdot 2\\pi =-\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\,✓\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{7\\pi }{3}-3\\cdot 2\\pi =-\\frac{11\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\nThere are no answer choices smaller than $-\\frac{11\\pi }{3},$ so we can stop here.\n\nSo the correct answer is $-\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186233362 1074 "To find the coterminal angles of a given angle $\\theta $ (in radians), we add and subtract integer multiples of $2\\pi :$\n\nLet's start by adding multiples of $2\\pi $ to $\\frac{7\\pi }{3}$:\n\n- $\\frac{7\\pi }{3}+2\\pi =\\frac{13\\pi }{3}$\n\nThere are no answer choices larger than $\\frac{13\\pi }{3},$ so we can stop here.\n\nNow, let's subtract multiples of $2\\pi $ from $\\frac{7\\pi }{3}$:\n\n- $\\frac{7\\pi }{3}-2\\pi =\\frac{\\pi }{3}$\n\n- $\\frac{7\\pi }{3}-2\\cdot 2\\pi =-\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\,✓$\n\n- $\\frac{7\\pi }{3}-3\\cdot 2\\pi =-\\frac{11\\pi }{3}$\n\nThere are no answer choices smaller than $-\\frac{11\\pi }{3},$ so we can stop here.\n\nSo the correct answer is $-\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186233363 1071 "Evaluate the geometric series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=4}^{\\infty }(0.75)^{n-3}.\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186233363 1071 "Evaluate the geometric series ${\\sum }_{n=4}^{\\infty }(0.75)^{n-3}.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186233371 1071 "Which of the following series are convergent?\n\nI. - $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{n\\sqrt[3]{{n}^{2}}}\n$$\nII. - $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{3{n}^{4}}\n$$\nIII. - $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{5\\sqrt[3]{n}}{\\sqrt{n}}\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186233371 1071 "Which of the following series are convergent?\n\nI. - ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{n\\sqrt[3]{{n}^{2}}}$\nII. - ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{3{n}^{4}}$\nIII. - ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{5\\sqrt[3]{n}}{\\sqrt{n}}$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186233371 1074 "We analyze each series in turn.\n\n- First, we look at $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{n\\sqrt[3]{{n}^{2}}}.\n$$ Rewriting this series gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{n\\sqrt[3]{{n}^{2}}} & =2{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{n\\cdot {n}^{2/3}} \\\\  & =2{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{5/3}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand this is a $p$-series with $p=\\frac{5}{3}.$ Since $p>1,$ the series converges by the $p$-series test.\n\n- For the second series, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{3{n}^{4}}=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{4}}\n$$\n\n \nwhich is a $p$-series with $p=4.$ Since $p>1,$ the series is convergent.\n\n- For the third series, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{5\\sqrt[3]{n}}{\\sqrt{n}} & =5{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{n}^{1/3}}{{n}^{1/2}} \\\\  & =5{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{1/6}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand this is a $p$-series with $p=\\frac{1}{6}.$ Since $p\\le 1,$ the series diverges by the $p$-series test.\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186233371 1074 "We analyze each series in turn.\n\n- First, we look at ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{n\\sqrt[3]{{n}^{2}}}.$ Rewriting this series gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{n\\sqrt[3]{{n}^{2}}} & =2{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{n\\cdot {n}^{2/3}} \\\\  & =2{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{5/3}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand this is a $p$-series with $p=\\frac{5}{3}.$ Since $p>1,$ the series converges by the $p$-series test.\n\n- For the second series, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{3{n}^{4}}=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{4}}\n$$\n\n \nwhich is a $p$-series with $p=4.$ Since $p>1,$ the series is convergent.\n\n- For the third series, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{5\\sqrt[3]{n}}{\\sqrt{n}} & =5{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{n}^{1/3}}{{n}^{1/2}} \\\\  & =5{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{1/6}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand this is a $p$-series with $p=\\frac{1}{6}.$ Since $p\\le 1,$ the series diverges by the $p$-series test.\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186233372 1071 "Which of the following series are convergent?\n\nI. - $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3n}{\\sqrt[4]{{n}^{5}}}\n$$\nII. - $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{5}{4{n}^{3}}\n$$\nIII. - $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{n}}{\\sqrt[5]{{n}^{3}}}\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186233372 1071 "Which of the following series are convergent?\n\nI. - ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3n}{\\sqrt[4]{{n}^{5}}}$\nII. - ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{5}{4{n}^{3}}$\nIII. - ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{n}}{\\sqrt[5]{{n}^{3}}}$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186233372 1074 "We analyze each series in turn.\n\n- First, we look at $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3n}{\\sqrt[4]{{n}^{5}}}.\n$$ Rewriting this series gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3n}{\\sqrt[4]{{n}^{5}}} & =3{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n}{{n}^{5/4}} \\\\  & =3{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{1/4}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand this is a $p$-series with $p=\\frac{1}{4}.$ Since $p\\le 1,$ the series diverges by the $p$-series test.\n\n- For the second series, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{5}{4{n}^{3}}=\\frac{5}{4}\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{3}}\n$$\n\n \nand this is a $p$-series with $p=3.$ Since $p>1,$ the series converges by the $p$-series test.\n\n- For the third series, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^