at lies in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n- $-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+2\\pi =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\,✓$\n\nSo the angle $-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ is coterminal with $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$ Let's draw this coterminal angle in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/b5/b5397fc107a7425c822a9d00a169fa7c3624a3e29e3a8266897d459f19ac1aee.png)\n\nWe see that $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ lies in quadrant II. Therefore, $-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ also lies in this quadrant." 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186234168 1074 "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees. \n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by $\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{10}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }\\right)={126}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore $\\frac{7\\pi }{10}$ is equivalent to ${126}^{∘},$ which lies in the $2\\text{nd}$ quadrant.\n\n![](images/89/89b5e2656378adb5d91c9f58c261e4beaae252b67819b904bf209f6ed8a5a25e.png)\n\nTo express $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{10}\\right)$ in terms of $\\operatorname{cot}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ we follow three steps:\n\n1\\. Find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n2\\. Calculate the value of the function for ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n3\\. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\n**Step 1**: Since $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{10}$ is in the $2$nd quadrant, the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\pi -\\theta  \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{7\\pi }{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{10}\\right)$, and therefore we're interested in $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cot}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{10}\\right).\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{10}\\right)$ must be negative because tangent (and therefore cotangent)