is ${\\int }_{4}^{9}\\operatorname{ln}{x}^{2/3}\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a left Riemann sum?" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186234342 1071 "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[{sec}^{2}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi l}{2n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2n}].\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186234342 1071 "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that ${\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[{sec}^{2}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi l}{2n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2n}].$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186234342 1074 "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{l})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{l}=a+lΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{\\pi }{2n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{2n}=\\frac{(b-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right))}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{l}=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi l}{2n}\n$$ and $f({x}_{l})={sec}^{2}⁡{x}_{l}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[{sec}^{2}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi l}{2n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x$ and $b=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186234342 1074 "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{l})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{l}=a+lΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{\\pi }{2n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{2n}=\\frac{(b-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right))}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{l}=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi l}{2n}$ and $f({x}_{l})={sec}^{2}⁡{x}_{l}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[{sec}^{2}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi l}{2n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x$ and $b=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186234344 1071 "What is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{4}^{7}(3x-7)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186234344 1071 "What is ${\\int }_{4}^{7}(3x-7)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186234356 1071 "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{2}^{z}}{\\sqrt{{2}^{z}-9}}\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186234356 1071 "Calculate the integral $\\int \\frac{{2}^{z}}{\\sqrt{{2}^{z}-9}}\\,\\text{d}z.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186234363 1071 "Find the eighth partial sum of the series\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(2n-4).\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186234363 1071 "Find the eighth partial sum of the series\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(2n-4).$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186234363 1074 "Let ${a}_{n}=2n-4.$ If we compute the first few terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =2(1)-4=-2 \\\\ {a}_{2} & =2(2)-4=0 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =2(3)-4=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we notice that this is an arithmetic sequence with common difference $d=2$ and first term ${a}_{1}=-2.$ \n\nWe can compute the $k$th partial sum of an arithmetic series using the formula\n\n$$\n